ed, but the ledger must\nnot impose that interpretation.\n\nThe record must remain useful to consumers beyond a single tool: cross-CLI\ncontinuity, archival/export, code attribution, review, and later intent\nretrieval are all valid independent uses.\n\n## Behavioral commitments\n\n- Preserve source material and provenance; do not silently rewrite history.\n- Preserve exact visible turns before creating optional derived views.\n- Retain enough provenance for consumers to determine applicability without\n  treating any record as timeless truth.\n- Record the repository, branch, commit, worktree state, author, timestamps,\n  and capture mechanism with each artifact whenever available.\n- Keep storage out of ordinary working-tree status views while retaining normal\n  Git durability and shareability.\n- Make sharing, retention, redaction, and deletion deliberate user choices.\n\n## Non-goals for the foundation\n\n- Deciding which historical statement is correct or intended.\n- Summarizing, ranking, embedding, or otherwise interpreting conversations.\n- Controlling an agent, enforcing a workflow, or orchestrating agents.\n- Decrypting, interpreting, or reproducing reasoning the provider hides.\n  Visible reasoning is recorded when exposed; reasoning withheld behind\n  provider ciphertext (0.10.0+) is preserved losslessly as opaque raw state\n  for a capable downstream consumer to decrypt later, but the ledger itself\n  never attempts to read or reconstruct it, and stores nothing that would let\n  it reproduce a native agent session on its own.\n\nThose belong to clients such as Turnbridge and Intent Recall, not to this core\nstorage layer.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md","numLines":41,"startLine":1,"totalLines":41},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"38a11272-1e3b-4a40-9b1d-8d13e2d449ce","version":"2.1.220"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-01T02:36:35.810Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# SCRATCH: `cledger redact` does not remove the secret\n2\t\n3\tWorking notes for whoever picks this up. Not a design doc — a problem\n4\tstatement, the measurements behind it, and the decision that has to be made\n5\tbefore any code is written.\n6\t\n7\tStatus 2026-07-31: **unfixed**. `cledger redact` reports success while the\n8\tredacted value remains recoverable with one git command.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. What was measured\n13\t\n14\tScratch repo, `redact.knownSecrets` on, value chosen so capture-tier\n15\tredaction would not catch it (so it lands raw and needs manual redaction):\n16\t\n17\t```\n18\t$ echo '{\"kind\":\"conversation_turn\", ... \"text\":\"my value is zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz ok\"}' \\\n19\t    | cledger append\n20\t$ cledger redact ev1-7bdf2121... --pattern zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz --reason test\n21\tcompanion event: ev1-279a0e320001\n22\thistory squashed: yes                      <-- claims success\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\tReads are clean:\n26\t\n27\t```\n28\t$ cledger log --all --json | grep -c zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz\n29\t0\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\tThe value is still there:\n33\t\n34\t```\n35\t$ for r in $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print $1}'); do\n36\t    git show \"$r\" | grep -q zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz && echo \"FOUND in $r\"\n37\t  done\n38\tFOUND in 8bdb47b\n39\tFOUND in 5e3d4ad\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\tIt goes away only after:\n43\t\n44\t```\n45\t$ git reflog expire --expire=now --all && git gc --prune=now\n46\t→ secret recoverable: 0\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\t**So the local purge is \"no reader surfaces it\", not \"it is gone\".** The\n50\tdefault reflog expiry window is 90 days.\n51\t\n52\tThere is a second, already-documented case that is worse and unaffected by any\n53\tof the above: if the notes ref was **already pushed**, `redactEvent` skips the\n54\tsquash entirely (it checks `ls-remote` first) and only warns. Nothing is\n55\tremoved anywhere, locally or on the remote.\n56\t\n57\t---\n58\t\n59\t## 2. Why the current mechanism cannot deliver redaction\n60\t\n61\t- A note's content lives in the **notes ref's own commit chain**, not on the\n62\t  commit it annotates.\n63\t- Every `git notes add -f` writes a new commit whose tree contains the new\n64\t  blob; the previous commit — and its blob — stays in the ancestry.\n65\t- `redactEvent` rewrites the event at the tip, then, if unpushed, rebuilds the\n66\t  ref as a parentless commit (`commit-tree` with no `-p`) so the old chain\n67\t  becomes unreachable **from the ref**.\n68\t- `update-ref` writes a reflog entry for every one of those discarded commits.\n69\t  Unreachable-from-ref is not unreachable-from-repo. That is the gap.\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 3. The load-bearing fact for any redesign\n74\t\n75\t**The notes tip is cumulative. Reading history is not required to see every\n76\tevent.** Verified:\n77\t\n78\t```\n79\t3 appends → 3 commits on refs/notes/conversation-ledger\n80\t$ git notes --ref=conversation-ledger show HEAD | wc -l\n81\t3                       <-- all three events present in the tip tree alone\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t`appendEvents` does `[...existing, ...fresh].sort()` and rewrites the whole\n85\tnote, so each anchor's note accumulates and the tip holds the complete event\n86\tset.\n87\t\n88\tThis matters because it dissolves what looks like a three-way conflict between\n89\t\"reads must see everything\", \"history must never be destroyed\", and \"redacted\n90\tbytes must be unrecoverable\". Those only conflict if reads need history — and\n91\tthey do not.\n92\t\n93\t**The only thing the notes ref's history contains that the tip does not is\n94\tsuperseded copies of rewritten note bodies — i.e. exactly the pre-redaction\n95\tsecrets.** A history-walking read would therefore add no conversations and\n96\twould surface every redacted value. (Concretely it would also hit\n97\t`dedupById`, whose tie-break prefers the earliest `recorded_at` — it would\n98\tactively *prefer* the un-redacted copy. That tie-break was written for\n99\tduplicate-anchor collapse, not for this, and must be revisited regardless.)\n100\t\n101\tIf the goal is that no evidence is ever lost, the tip already satisfies it.\n102\tDiscarding the notes ref's internal commit history loses **zero events**.\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## 4. Decision required before implementing\n107\t\n108\tBoth options below end in the same security state; they differ only in whether\n109\tthe notes ref keeps its own commit chain.\n110\t\n111\t**Option A — keep the squash, finish the job.** After the existing\n112\tsquash-to-parentless-commit: expire the ledger ref's reflog, then prune. Small\n113\tchange to existing code. Loses the notes ref's internal commit history, which\n114\tcarries no unique events (§3).\n115\t\n116\t**Option B — rewrite the chain.** Rebuild every commit in the notes ref with a\n117\ttree that has the secret removed, preserving the chain shape (filter-branch\n118\tshaped, scoped to one ref). Keeps a commit-by-commit audit trail of the ledger\n119\tref itself. Strictly more work, same outcome for the secret. Still needs the\n120\treflog expiry and prune from Option A — rewriting leaves the originals behind\n121\texactly the same way.\n122\t\n123\t**Recommendation: A**, unless there is a stated reason to keep the notes ref's\n124\town commit chain. B's only benefit is auditing the ledger's storage layer,\n125\twhich is not evidence about the repo and is not what the project promises to\n126\tpreserve.\n127\t\n128\t**The blocking sub-question either way:** reclaiming the objects needs a\n129\tprune, and `git gc --prune=now` is repo-wide — it would also drop unreachable\n130\tobjects the user may want (dropped stashes, abandoned commits). Choices:\n131\t\n132\t1. scoped `git reflog expire --expire=now refs/notes/conversation-ledger`\n133\t   plus an accepted repo-wide prune;\n134\t2. scoped reflog expiry only,