{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Read the whole source tree (~3.5k lines across 20 modules), both docs, and verified the security-critical claims by running the built CLI in throwaway repos. Here's the rundown.\n\n---\n\n# 1. The model in one paragraph\n\ncledger captures the visible transcript of coding-agent sessions and stores each turn as one JSON line inside a **git note attached to whatever commit was HEAD when capture ran**. Notes live on their own ref, `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, so nothing ever appears in your working tree. Because events are keyed to commits, conversations ride the commit DAG for free: merge a branch, and its conversations become visible from the target. Reading = enumerate notes, filter by `git rev-list` reachability. Sharing = push that one ref.\n\n# 2. The substrate: git notes, plainly\n\nThis is the piece most people get wrong, and several of the project's sharp edges fall directly out of it.\n\nA notes ref is **its own commit chain**, parallel to your code history. Each commit on it has a tree that maps `commit-SHA → blob`. The blob is the note body.\n\nThree consequences that drive everything:\n\n- **The tip is the whole database.** Not an append log — a snapshot. The tip commit's tree contains the note for *every* annotated commit. This is why reads never walk notes history, and why pushing a filtered tree would silently delete collaborators' notes (`pushScopedNotes` works around this by seeding a temp ref from the remote's tip and unioning into it, so it only ever adds).\n- **Every write is a new commit; old blobs stay in the ancestry.** `git notes add -f` rewrites the whole note. The previous blob remains reachable from the chain. This is the root of the redaction problem in §8.\n- **Merging is line-based.** `notes.conversation-ledger.mergeStrategy=cat_sort_uniq` is set on first append. Concurrent appends union cleanly with zero conflicts — concatenate lines, sort, dedupe. That's why the note body is sorted unique JSONL. It's also why a *rewritten* line and its original both survive a merge: they're different bytes, so `uniq` keeps both.\n\nAnchoring is to **HEAD at capture time**. `appendEvents` reads the existing note lines, filters new events by id, re-sorts everything, and rewrites the note wholesale.\n\n# 3. What one event is\n\n`src/schema.ts`. Every event carries:\n\n| field | in identity hash? | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `kind` | ✅ | `conversation_turn`, `reasoning`, `unrecognized`, `redaction`, `re_anchor`, `supersession`, … (open string) |\n| `occurred_at` | ✅ | from the source transcript |\n| `actor.type` / `actor.id` | ✅ | human → git author email; agent → model id |\n| `producer.source` / `session_id` | ✅ | `claude-code` / `codex` + native session uuid |\n| `conversation.id` / `.seq` | ✅ | `<source>:<uuid>` + **source line index** |\n| `content` | ✅ | the converted visible turn |\n| `links`, `media_type` | ✅ | |\n| `recorded_at` | ❌ | append time |\n| `context` | ❌ | repo/branch/HEAD/cwd/dirty fingerprint |\n| `raw` | ❌ | **the original source line, verbatim** |\n| `producer.model`/`provider`/`source_version` | ❌ | added in 0.12.0, excluded so the upgrade didn't churn every id |\n| `redactions[]` | ❌ | audit trail of capture-time scrubs |\n\n**`id = \"ev1-\" + sha256(canonical JSON of the ticked fields)`.** So the id is a content hash of the source material. Re-scan the same transcript → identical id → dedup. That's what makes capture idempotent and re-runnable, and it's load-bearing for redaction (§8).\n\nThe thing to internalize: **`content` is inside the hash.** Change the content, change the id. Redaction has to fight this explicitly.\n\n# 4. Capture — when, what, where\n\n`cledger install` writes hooks into the *coding CLIs*, globally:\n\n- **Claude Code** → `Stop` + `SessionEnd` entries in `~/.claude/settings.json`\n- **Codex** → `[[hooks.Stop]]` in `~/.codex/config.toml`\n\nOn fire, the CLI pipes `{session_id, transcript_path, cwd}` on stdin. cledger reads the harness's own transcript file — `~/.claude/projects/<escaped-cwd>/<session>.jsonl` or `~/.codex/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-*.jsonl` — from a saved cursor (a line count) to EOF.\n\nEach line is triaged:\n\n- **Convertible** (`user`/`assistant`; codex `message`/`function_call`/`function_call_output`/`agent_message`) → a `conversation_turn`\n- **Known-skipped** (session bookkeeping, UI state) → dropped\n- **Anything else** → an `unrecognized` event whose `content` is just a type label and whose `raw.data` is the full line — deliberately preserved so a later adapter version can re-interpret it via `cledger renormalize`\n- **Codex `reasoning`** → a `reasoning` event; the `encrypted_content` ciphertext is preserved byte-exact and never interpreted\n\n**Critical for the security story:** every event stores the turn *twice* — once converted into `content`, once verbatim under `raw.data`. And `content` includes **tool inputs and tool results**. `cat .env`, a curl with an auth header, an env dump, a stack trace with a connection string — all of it, in full, both times.\n\n# 5. The keying map\n\n```\nevent ──anchored to──▶ HEAD commit at capture time      (the git-side key)\n      ──conversation.id──▶ \"claude-code:<uuid>\" | \"codex:<session_id>\"\n      ──conversation.seq─▶ source transcript line index  (ordering + distinctness)\n      ──producer.session_id▶ native session id\n      ──actor.id──────────▶ git author email (humans) | model id (claude assistant)\n      ──context───────────▶ {repo origin URL, branch, HEAD sha, cwd,\n                             sha256(git status --porcelain) if dirty}\ncursor ──keyed by──▶ session id, one file per session\n```\n\nNote `conversation.seq` is the **line index in the source file**, not a turn counter. That's what makes ids stable across rescans and what lets `renormalize` reconstruct a turn with a byte-identical id.\n\n# 6. Local state — all rebuildable, never the record of truth\n\nEverything under `<git-common-dir>/conversation-ledger/`. The *common* dir, not the per-worktree one — that was a real bug fixed in 0.13.0 (worktrees behaved like separate repos; the hook never even ran).\n\n| file | what |\n|---|---|\n| `pending.jsonl` | events captured before the repo's first commit; flushed into the first real anchor |\n| `cursors/<session>.json` | how far into each transcript we've read |\n| `lock/` | mkdir-based mutex, 10s then stolen |\n| `allowlist.json` | scan fingerprints marked false-positive |\n| **`known-secrets.json`** | **plaintext secrets, mode 0600** — see §8 |\n| `reanchor-cursor` | last-seen target branch tip |\n| `transport-warnings.json` | once-per-repo warning suppression |\n\nConfig merges `~/.config/cledger/config.json` then `<repo>/.cledger.json`, repo wins **per whole top-level section** — not a deep merge. And `readJsonConfig` returns `null` on any parse error, so **a typo'd config silently reverts to defaults** with no warning. That's the #1 roadmap item and it's a fair one: it fails open on a file whose entire job is turning safety features on.\n\n# 7. Distribution\n\nWired automatically on first capture (`ensureTransport`, re-checked every append, never throws):\n\n- **Push:** a marker-delimited block appended to `<common-dir>/hooks/pre-push`. It reads git's pre-push stdin to learn which refs you're pushing, then runs `cledger transport-push`. Scoped since 0.14.0 — pushing a branch shares only that branch's conversations. A `CLEDGER_INTERNAL` env guard prevents recursion.\n- **Fetch:** a refspec `+refs/notes/conversation-ledger:refs/notes/cledger-incoming` added to `remote.origin.fetch`, so plain `git fetch` stages the remote's ref. `absorbIncoming` folds it into the local ref lazily — **on every single read** — via `cat_sort_uniq`.\n\nThat last detail matters in §8: reads mutate your local ledger.\n\n---\n\n# 8. The complete lifespan of potentially insecure information\n\nThis is the part you asked for specifically. Stages 0–4 are as designed; 5 and 6 are where it breaks, and I verified both by running the tool.\n\n### Stage 0 — Origin (unprotected, by explicit design)\n\nA secret enters the session: you paste a key, or a tool result contains one. It is now in the **harness's own transcript file**, plaintext, on disk. cledger has done nothing and will never touch that file. The stated threat model: *\"the trust boundary is transport\"* — redaction protects the shared record, not local disk. Worth knowing that's a deliberate choice, not an oversight.\n\n### Stage 1 — Capture-time scrubbing (layers A/C/D/F)\n\nInside `appendEvents`, **before ids are computed**, a deep-walk over `content` and `raw.data` (string leaves only, never object keys) applies, in order:\n\n- **F. known-secrets** (opt-in): exact-match values a previous redaction confirmed\n- **C. env values** (opt-in): `process.env` values ≥8 chars + parsed `.env`\n- **A. capture ruleset** (default on): 13 regexes for *prefixed* tokens — `ghp_`, `github_pat_`, `sk-ant-`, `sk-proj-`, `AKIA`, `AIza`, `xox[baprs]-`, `sk_live_`, `glpat-`, `npm_`, `SG.`, PEM blocks, JWTs\n- **D. user regexes** from config\n\nMatches become `[REDACTED:<rule>:<sha256(secret)[:12]>]`, with a `redactions[]` audit entry. One exemption: `reasoning` events' `*/encrypted_content` is never pattern-matched (a coincidental hit would silently corrupt ciphertext that exists to be replayed).\n\n**What layer A does not catch: anything unprefixed.** Passwords, DB connection strings, internal/bespoke tokens, session cookies, private URLs. This is intentional — a capture-tier false positive silently rewrites the permanent record — but it means most real-world leaked material sails straight through into the note.\n\n### Stage 2 — Storage\n\nEvent finalized, id computed, canonical JSON appended to the note on HEAD via `git notes add -f`. New commit on the notes ref. The secret (if A missed it) is now a git blob, GC-protected.\n\n### Stage 3 — The sync gate (layer E)\n\nOn `git push` (via hook) or `cledger sync`. This is the real checkpoint.\n\nScans **only events the remote doesn't already have**, and only those **in push scope**. Tiers: `standard` = capture rules re-run + `keyword-assignment` + `url-credentials` + `bearer-token`; `paranoid` adds an entropy-gated high-entropy-token rule. Allowlisted fingerprints suppressed.\n\nAny surviving finding → **push blocked**. Default policy holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed; `transport.strict: true` aborts everything.\n\nThe report design here is genuinely good and worth preserving: **findings print coordinates only** — event id, rule, `path@offset`, fingerprint. No excerpt, ever. Because the *context around* a match is what tripped the rule, printing it into an agent session mints a new event that trips the same rule, and findings compound on every scan. They hit this in the wild. Reading is quarantined into `cledger inspect`, which writes to a 0600 file outside the repo and **refuses to run inside an agent session** (detects `CLAUDECODE`/`CODEX_` env markers). That guard is load-bearing: an agent asked to investigate a blocked sync will otherwise helpfully read the secret straight into the record being protected.\n\n### Stage 4 — Remediation\n\n`cledger redact <id> --pattern REGEX` rewrites the note line in place. The subtle, correct bit: **the id is forced to stay identical to the original**. If it were recomputed from the scrubbed content, a later transcript rescan would regenerate the original event under its old id, find no match, and re-add the secret. Keeping the id pinned means the rewritten line permanently occupies that slot. A companion `redaction` event records why content no longer matches its hash.\n\nThen, if the notes ref has never been pushed, it squashes the notes ref to a parentless commit.\n\n### Stage 5 — ❌ The squash doesn't remove anything (verified)\n\nI ran this. Scratch repo, secret that layer A won't catch:\n\n```\n$ cledger redact ev1-a44c633a --pattern zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz --reason test\nhistory squashed: yes                    ← claims success\n$ cledger log --all --json | grep -c zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz\n0                                        ← reads are clean\n$ git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger\ncd956c3 @{0}:    f2de5b7 @{1}:    cc51ca5 @{2}:    4b7b8cd @{3}:\n→ git show cc51ca5 | grep -q SECRET      FOUND\n→ git show 4b7b8cd | grep -q SECRET      FOUND\n→ blob e4adcb8f… still in object store   FOUND\n```\n\n`update-ref` writes a reflog entry for every discarded commit. Unreachable-*from-ref* is not unreachable-*from-repo*. Only `git reflog expire --expire=now --all && git gc --prune=now` actually removes it (I confirmed that clears it). Default reflog retention is 90 days.\n\nSo `history squashed: yes` asserts an outcome the tool does not achieve. The scratch doc's central claim is **correct**.\n\n**One thing the scratch doc misses:** with `knownSecrets` on — **and your global `~/.config/cledger/config.json` has it on** — `cledger redact --pattern` also writes the plaintext to `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`:\n\n```json\n{ \"values\": [ \"zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz\" ] }\n```\n\nThat's by design (exact-matching needs plaintext; fingerprints are one-way), it's 0600, and it's under `.git/` so it never pushes. But the net effect of running \"redact\" today is that plaintext copies on disk go from **one to two** — three counting the untouched harness transcript.\n\n### Stage 6 — ❌ Post-push: the redaction gets silently reversed (verified, new)\n\nIf the notes ref already reached the remote, `redactEvent` skips the squash and just warns. The scratch doc says \"nothing is removed anywhere.\" **It's worse than that.**\n\n`redactEvent` builds the rewritten event by spreading the original — which carries `recorded_at` along with it. So the redacted copy and the original have **the same id and the same `recorded_at`**. Now recall that `cat_sort_uniq` unions *lines*, and those two lines differ in bytes, so a merge keeps both. `dedupById` collapses them: earliest `recorded_at` wins — a tie — falling through to the tie-break, `canonicalJson(a) < canonicalJson(b)`. A **raw byte comparison**, decided at the first differing character: the secret's first byte versus `[` (0x5B) from the placeholder.\n\nFull scenario, run end to end: you push, a colleague fetches the original, you redact, they push anything at all, you `git fetch` — and `absorbIncoming` (which runs on *every read*) merges their copy back in.\n\n```\nPost-push redact + peer divergence, varying the secret's first character:\n  starts 'A' (0x41)   -> REVERSED (plaintext back in reads)\n  starts '9' (0x39)   -> REVERSED (plaintext back in reads)\n  starts 'z' (0x7A)   -> held (redacted copy won)\n```\n\nAnd the read output after reversal:\n\n```\n2026-07-31T00:00:00Z  conversation_turn  user  key AATOPSECRETVALUE99 end\n2026-08-01T02:33:41Z  redaction          human {\"mode\":\"pattern\",\"reason\":\"test\",...}\n```\n\n`cledger log` prints the plaintext again — next to the `redaction` event still asserting it was redacted. No warning.\n\nSince bytes below `0x5B` include **every digit and every uppercase letter**, this hits roughly half of realistic secrets, and specifically the uppercase/digit-leading ones. The design doc flags `dedupById` as a hazard only for hypothetical history-walking reads; in fact it's reachable today through the ordinary fetch path.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Honest assessment\n\n**What's genuinely well-built** — and I'd urge you not to throw out while fixing the rest:\n\n- The storage model. Anchoring to commits and deriving branch membership from the DAG at read time is the right call, and the doc's defense of it against per-branch refs is correct (squash merge is the decisive case).\n- Content-hash identity making capture idempotent. Elegant, and it makes the whole hook-fires-repeatedly design safe.\n- Id pinning during redaction. Subtle and right.\n- The coordinates-only scan report plus the `inspect` agent-session refusal. That's a real failure mode, correctly diagnosed and well fixed.\n- The scoped push seeding from the remote's tip. Non-obvious and correct.\n\n**What's actually broken:**\n\n1. **Post-push redaction is reversible** via ordinary fetch+merge, ~half the time, silently. This is the most severe issue and it is *not* in the docs. `dedupById` must prefer the redacted copy — the presence of a `redactions[]` entry, or the companion `redaction` event, should decide, never a byte compare.\n2. **`history squashed: yes` is a false claim.** Either finish the job (scoped reflog expire + prune, with the repo-wide-prune question answered) or stop asserting it.\n3. **`redact` increases the plaintext copy count on disk** when `knownSecrets` is on, which is your current global default.\n4. **Config fails open silently.** A malformed `.cledger.json` disables every protection you thought you enabled, with no message.\n\nThe scratch doc is accurate on what it covers and its Option A recommendation is sound — but it under-states the severity, because it frames the problem as \"recoverable by deliberate digging\" when the post-push case is actually \"printed by an ordinary read.\" I'd fix `dedupById` first; it's the smallest change with the largest security delta, and it's independent of the reflog/prune decision.\n\nI made no changes to the repo — this was read-only plus throwaway scratch repos, since cleaned up.\n\nresult: Delivered a verified architecture + secret-lifecycle rundown of conversation-ledger; confirmed the scratch doc's reflog gap and found a more severe undocumented flaw — post-push redactions are silently reversed by ordinary fetch/merge because `dedupById`'s tie-break is a raw byte compare that favors the unredacted copy for any secret starting with a digit or uppercase letter.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"f6c0bab7a93d68dd6aff2cb6b3ba0ce3d79f3e89","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:a4ea5e90-d87a-4bf6-a351-9d8227a2ef59","seq":161},"id":"ev1-eee8b1c17d370190207e534429c491f03ec49685a8c7ab5da4e25fa625a19691","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-01T02:36:35.657Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"a4ea5e90-d87a-4bf6-a351-9d8227a2ef59","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.220","tool":"cledger","version":"0.16.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Read the whole source tree (~3.5k lines across 20 modules), both docs, and verified the security-critical claims by running the built CLI in throwaway repos. Here's the rundown.\n\n---\n\n# 1. The model in one paragraph\n\ncledger captures the visible transcript of coding-agent sessions and stores each turn as one JSON line inside a **git note attached to whatever commit was HEAD when capture ran**. Notes live on their own ref, `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, so nothing ever appears in your working tree. Because events are keyed to commits, conversations ride the commit DAG for free: merge a branch, and its conversations become visible from the target. Reading = enumerate notes, filter by `git rev-list` reachability. Sharing = push that one ref.\n\n# 2. The substrate: git notes, plainly\n\nThis is the piece most people get wrong, and several of the project's sharp edges fall directly out of it.\n\nA notes ref is **its own commit chain**, parallel to your code history. Each commit on it has a tree that maps `commit-SHA → blob`. The blob is the note body.\n\nThree consequences that drive everything:\n\n- **The tip is the whole database.** Not an append log — a snapshot. The tip commit's tree contains the note for *every* annotated commit. This is why reads never walk notes history, and why pushing a filtered 