.\n\n**What layer A does not catch: anything unprefixed.** Passwords, DB connection strings, internal/bespoke tokens, session cookies, private URLs. This is intentional — a capture-tier false positive silently rewrites the permanent record — but it means most real-world leaked material sails straight through into the note.\n\n### Stage 2 — Storage\n\nEvent finalized, id computed, canonical JSON appended to the note on HEAD via `git notes add -f`. New commit on the notes ref. The secret (if A missed it) is now a git blob, GC-protected.\n\n### Stage 3 — The sync gate (layer E)\n\nOn `git push` (via hook) or `cledger sync`. This is the real checkpoint.\n\nScans **only events the remote doesn't already have**, and only those **in push scope**. Tiers: `standard` = capture rules re-run + `keyword-assignment` + `url-credentials` + `bearer-token`; `paranoid` adds an entropy-gated high-entropy-token rule. Allowlisted fingerprints suppressed.\n\nAny surviving finding → **push blocked**. Default policy holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed; `transport.strict: true` aborts everything.\n\nThe report design here is genuinely good and worth preserving: **findings print coordinates only** — event id, rule, `path@offset`, fingerprint. No excerpt, ever. Because the *context around* a match is what tripped the rule, printing it into an agent session mints a new event that trips the same rule, and findings compound on every scan. They hit this in the wild. Reading is quarantined into `cledger inspect`, which writes to a 0600 file outside the repo and **refuses to run inside an agent session** (detects `CLAUDECODE`/`CODEX_` env markers). That guard is load-bearing: an agent asked to investigate a blocked sync will otherwise helpfully read the secret straight into the record being protected.\n\n### Stage 4 — Remediation\n\n`cledger redact <id> --pattern REGEX` rewrites the note line in place. The subtle, correct bit: **the id is forced to stay identical to the original**. If it were recomputed from the scrubbed content, a later transcript rescan would regenerate the original event under its old id, find no match, and re-add the secret. Keeping the id pinned means the rewritten line permanently occupies that slot. A companion `redaction` event records why content no longer matches its hash.\n\nThen, if the notes ref has never been pushed, it squashes the notes ref to a parentless commit.\n\n### Stage 5 — ❌ The squash doesn't remove anything (verified)\n\nI ran this. Scratch repo, secret that layer A won't catch:\n\n```\n$ cledger redact ev1-a44c633a --pattern zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz --reason test\nhistory squashed: yes                    ← claims success\n$ cledger log --all --json | grep -c zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz\n0                                        ← reads are clean\n$ git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger\ncd956c3 @{0}:    f2de5b7 @{1}:    cc51ca5 @{2}:    4b7b8cd @{3}:\n→ git show cc51ca5 | grep -q SECRET      FOUND\n→ git show 4b7b8cd | grep -q SECRET      FOUND\n→ blob e4adcb8f… still in object store   FOUND\n```\n\n`update-ref` writes a reflog entry for every discarded commit. Unreachable-*from-ref* is not unreachable-*from-repo*. Only `git reflog expire --expire=now --all && git gc --prune=now` actually removes it (I confirmed that clears it). Default reflog retention is 90 days.\n\nSo `history squashed: yes` asserts an outcome the tool does not achieve. The scratch doc's central claim is **correct**.\n\n**One thing the scratch doc misses:** with `knownSecrets` on — **and your global `~/.config/cledger/config.json` has it on** — `cledger redact --pattern` also writes the plaintext to `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`:\n\n```json\n{ \"values\": [ \"zzTOPSECRETVALUEzz\" ] }\n```\n\nThat's by design (exact-matching needs plaintext; fingerprints are one-way), it's 0600, and it's under `.git/` so it never pushes. But the net effect of running \"redact\" today is that plaintext copies on disk go from **one to two** — three counting the untouched harness transcript.\n\n### Stage 6 — ❌ Post-push: the redaction gets silently reversed (verified, new)\n\nIf the notes ref already reached the remote, `redactEvent` skips the squash and just warns. The scratch doc says \"nothing is removed anywhere.\" **It's worse than that.**\n\n`redactEvent` builds the rewritten event by spreading the original — which carries `recorded_at` along with it. So the redacted copy and the original have **the same id and the same `recorded_at`**. Now recall that `cat_sort_uniq` unions *lines*, and those two lines differ in bytes, so a merge keeps both. `dedupById` collapses them: earliest `recorded_at` wins — a tie — falling through to the tie-break, `canonicalJson(a) < canonicalJson(b)`. A **raw byte comparison**, decided at the first differing character: the secret's first byte versus `[` (0x5B) from the placeholder.\n\nFull scenario, run end to end: you push, a colleague fetches the original, you redact, they push anything at all, you `git fetch` — and `absorbIncoming` (which runs on *every read*) merges their copy back in.\n\n```\nPost-push redact + peer divergence, varying the secret's first character:\n  starts 'A' (0x41)   -> REVERSED (plaintext back in reads)\n  starts '9' (0x39)   -> REVERSED (plaintext back in reads)\n  starts 'z' (0x7A)   -> held (redacted copy won)\n```\n\nAnd the read output after reversal:\n\n```\n2026-07-31T00:00:00Z  conversation_turn  user  key AATOPSECRETVALUE99 end\n2026-08-01T02:33:41Z  redaction          human {\"mode\":\"pattern\",\"reason\":\"test\",...}\n```\n\n`cledger log` prints the plaintext again — next to the `redaction` event still asserting it was redacted. No warning.\n\nSince bytes below `0x5B` include **every digit and every uppercase letter**, this hits roughly half of realistic secrets, and specifically the uppercase/digit-leading ones. The design doc flags `dedupById` as a hazard only for hypothetical history-walking reads; in fact it's reachable today through the ordinary fetch path.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Honest assessment\n\n**What's genuinely well-built** — and I'd urge you not to throw out while fixing the rest:\n\n- The storage model. Anchoring to commits and deriving branch membership from the DAG at read time is the right call, and the doc's defense of it against per-branch refs is correct (squash merge is the decisive case).\n- Content-hash identity making capture idempotent. Elegant, and it makes the whole hook-fires-repeatedly design safe.\n- Id pinning during redaction. Subtle and right.\n- The coordinates-only scan report plus the `inspect` agent-session refusal. That's a real failure mode, correctly diagnosed and well fixed.\n- The scoped push seeding from the remote's tip. Non-obvious and correct.\n\n**What's actually broken:**\n\n1. **Post-push redaction is reversible** via ordinary fetch+merge, ~half the time, silently. This is the most severe issue and it is *not* in the docs. `dedupById` must prefer the redacted copy — the presence of a `redactions[]` entry, or the companion `redaction` event, should decide, never a byte compare.\n2. **`history squashed: yes` is a false claim.** Either finish the job (scoped reflog expire + prune, with the repo-wide-prune question answered) or stop asserting it.\n3. **`redact` increases the plaintext copy count on disk** when `knownSecrets` is on, which is your current global default.\n4. **Config fails open silently.** A malformed `.cledger.json` disables every protection you thought you enabled, with no message.\n\nThe scratch doc is accurate on what it covers and its Option A recommendation is sound — but it under-states the severity, because it frames the problem as \"recoverable by deliberate digging\" when the post-push case is actually \"printed by an ordinary read.\" I'd fix `dedupById` first; it's the smallest change with the largest security delta, and it's independent of the reflog/prune decision.\n\nI made no changes to the repo — this was read-only plus throwaway scratch repos, since cleaned up.\n\nresult: Delivered a verified architecture + secret-lifecycle rundown of conversation-ledger; confirmed the scratch doc's reflog gap and found a more severe undocumented flaw — post-push redactions are silently reversed by ordinary fetch/merge because `dedupById`'s tie-break is a raw byte compare that favors the unredacted copy for any secret starting with a digit or uppercase letter.","type":"text"}],"diagnostics":null,"id":"msg_011CdbFNKBudhnA9ESoTySvC","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":966,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":966,"cache_read_input_tokens":150943,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"iterations":[{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":966,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":966,"cache_read_input_tokens":150943,"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6648,"type":"message"}],"output_tokens":6648,"server_tool_use":{"web_fetch_requests":0,"web_search_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","speed":"standard"}},"parentUuid":"bfeb0da6-0eb4-4a61-9fa8-357d25c47fb5","requestId":"req_011CdbFNHMXU8VQg4NKESumF","sessionId":"a4ea5e90-d87a-4bf6-a351-9d8227a2ef59","sessionKind":"bg","session_id":"a4ea5e90-d87a-4bf6-a351-9d8227a2ef59","timestamp":"2026-08-01T02:36:35.657Z","type":"assistant","userType":"external","uuid":"383745f1-ff46-4b5d-bc4e-862ea5c17b59","version":"2.1.220"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-03T02:48:00.730Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
