{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"`docs/SCRATCH-redaction-does-not-redact.md`, on main (`f6c0bab`), pushed.\n\nIt contains: the repro with exact commands and output, why the mechanism can't deliver redaction, the two implementation options, the blocking prune-scope question, and the things that need fixing regardless (the false `history squashed: yes`, the `dedupById` tie-break, the already-pushed path).\n\nTwo things in it that push back on your framing, which you should know are there before you hand it to the next agent:\n\n**The notes tip is cumulative — reads never need history.** I verified this: 3 appends produce 3 commits on the notes ref, and all 3 events are present in the tip tree alone. `appendEvents` rewrites the whole note each time (`[...existing, ...fresh].sort()`), so each anchor's note accumulates.\n\nThe consequence matters for the fix: **the only thing the ref's history holds that the tip doesn't is superseded copies of rewritten notes — i.e. exactly the pre-redaction secrets.** So O(entire notes history) reads would recover zero additional conversations and would surface every redacted value. And discarding the notes ref's internal commit chain loses zero events, which is why I still recommend the squash path rather than a filter-branch-style rewrite. Both options are written up either way, so you can overrule it.\n\nThat's a factual claim, not a preference — if the next agent finds a case where the tip is not cumulative, the recommendation collapses and Option B becomes correct. Worth having them check it rather than take it from the doc.\n\nOn the `dedupById` tie-break: it isn't currently reachable, since nothing reads history. It was written for collapsing duplicate anchors after the worktree cursor bug, and preferring the earliest `recorded_at` was the wrong default to pick for a system that also rewrites events. It's flagged as a latent trap regardless of which option you take.\n\nOne correction to